nd interpretations (preserved, not silently resolved)\n226\t\n227\t- **C1 Stage order.** The original orders the stages conformance → design →\n228\t  source. In this lineage the CLI was measured and corrected before this\n229\t  conformance or any design existed. The contract permits this (\"Do not force\n230\t  graph names or a complete schedule\"). The correction's authority is the\n231\t  original source plus the baseline evidence, not this document. The\n232\t  implementation design must still treat this document as its authority (REQ-08)\n233\t  and must be checked against the current CLI. Residual: the assessor judges\n234\t  whether the non-canonical order leaves any stage meaning unmet.\n235\t- **C2 `test-execution-result.json`.** The original `test_execution_plan` stage\n236\t  says to write `test-execution-result.json` and have the worker execute\n237\t  (\"EXECUTION-DEFAULT LAW\"). The contract realizes execution-receipt mechanics\n238\t  through admitted C2 evidence, keeps that file outside application write\n239\t  territory, and says a fabricated success file or an author's report is\n240\t  insufficient. The oracle's `requiredArtifacts` omit that file. Interpretation:\n241\t  the result meaning is kept (REQ-11 and AC-12), and admitted C2 observations\n242\t  carry it. No authored asset may substitute a handwritten result.\n243\t- **C3 Who executes.** The original has the plan author run the tests. Under the\n244\t  contract, measurement is a separate admitted C2 act. Any run the plan author\n245\t  makes themselves is not admitted evidence.\n246\t- **C4 One command or two.** The original plan schema has one\n247\t  `command`/`args` pair (the test command). The contract fixes two prospective\n248\t  commands and says the plan \"must agree with these commands\".\n249\t  Interpretation (an F_P judgment for the assessor):\n250\t  - the top-level `command`/`args` must be exactly the test command;\n251\t  - the plan must also declare the direct CLI invocation `node`\n252\t    `[\"generated/hello-world.mjs\"]` with expected stdout `\"Hello, world!\\n\"` and\n253\t    exit `0`, in an additional field that does not change the original fields'\n254\t    meaning.\n255\t- **C5 `assertedReturnValue` and the newline.** `assertedReturnValue` is\n256\t  `\"Hello, world!\"` with no newline, while stdout is `\"Hello, world!\\n\"`.\n257\t  Interpretation: `assertedReturnValue` is the greeting value, and the required\n258\t  stdout is that value plus exactly one LF (AC-12). This does not relax REQ-03.\n259\t- **C6 UAT status assertion.** The `uat_test_source` instructions require only an\n260\t  output assertion. `test_design` requires both tests to assert status `0`. The\n261\t  stricter combined reading applies (REQ-07). The supplied UAT test asserts both.\n262\t- **C7 \"From the project root\".** The supplied tests pass the relative path\n263\t  `generated/hello-world.mjs` and set no `cwd`, so they depend on being run with\n264\t  cwd = project root. Both fixed commands therefore require cwd = project root.\n265\t  The admitted observations record `relativeCwd: \".\"`. Their stack traces show\n266\t  execution inside an archived sandbox copy under `.ai-workspace/`, and file\n267\t  identity is tied to the worksite by the recorded dependency digests. Residual:\n268\t  the assessor judges whether equal digests are enough to treat the sandbox as\n269\t  the project root.\n270\t- **C8 Pass count without pinning.** `expectedTestPassCount` ≥ 2 is a floor. The\n271\t  runner-summary fragments in `expectedStdoutMatch` must not pin `pass 2`,\n272\t  because the original anticipates added depth coverage. In this run the test\n273\t  sources are protected, so the actual count is 2.\n274\t\n275\t## 9. Unknowns and unspecified behavior\n276\t\n277\t- **U1 Node version.** No minimum is stated in the source, contract or oracle,\n278\t  and none is recorded in the admitted observations. The author's local\n279\t  `node --version` returned `v24